<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Humphrey Gilbert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1539–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sir Humphrey Gilbert, Gilbert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Humphrey Gilbert was an English soldier and explorer of the Elizabethan era. In 1583 he made a formal claim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> for the English crown, but was lost at sea on the return voyage and left no settlers. His claim is conventionally treated as the founding moment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert.jpg" alt="Humphrey Gilbert" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Humphrey_Gilbert_Compton_Castle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Humphrey Gilbert was the fifth son of Otho Gilbert of Compton, Greenway and Galmpton in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a> and of Catherine Champernowne. He was a maternal half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh">Sir Walter Raleigh</a>. Gilbert was educated at Eton College and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where he learned French and Spanish and studied war and navigation, and his mentor was Sir Henry Sidney. He was wounded at the siege of Newhaven, in Le Havre, in June 1563.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1566 Gilbert served in Ireland under Sidney against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shane-oneill">Shane O'Neill</a>, and after O'Neill's assassination in 1567 he became governor of Ulster. In 1569 he took part in the fighting that followed the first Desmond Rebellion, capturing thirty to forty castles in Kerry and Connello. Thomas Churchyard recorded that he had the heads of the dead laid along the path to his tent to terrify the people. Sidney knighted him in 1570, and he sat in the English Parliament for Plymouth in 1571.</p><p>In 1566 he argued before Queen Elizabeth for a Northwest Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Cathay</a>, and Martin Frobisher's voyage of 1576 grew from the support he won. In 1573 he proposed an academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, which Sir Thomas Gresham eventually realised as Gresham College. After letters patent of 11 June 1578 he sailed with seven vessels that November, but storms scattered the fleet and forced it back some six months later. Failed expeditions exhausted his family fortune.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In June 1583 Gilbert sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> with five vessels. On 5 August 1583 he took possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/newfoundland">Newfoundland</a> for the English Crown at St. John's, by cutting turf in the manner of English common law. He claimed authority over the fish stations and taxed the fishermen. The ship Delight then ran aground on Sable Island after he ordered a controversial change of course, and only sixteen of its crew survived. He decided on 31 August to turn back.</p><p>On 9 September his small ship, the Squirrel, was nearly overwhelmed by heavy seas near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a>. Gilbert refused to move to the larger Golden Hind and was seen reading in the stern. At midnight the Squirrel's lights went out, and the ship was lost with all hands. A. L. Rowse described him as passionate, impulsive and liable to violence and cruelty, but also visionary and original. His claim was realised in 1610, and the royal charter granted to Raleigh in 1584 drew in part on his patent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Humphrey Gilbert” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its founding moment is conventionally placed at <b>Sir Humphrey Gilbert</b>'s formal claim to Newfoundland in 1583, though Gilbert was lost at sea on the return voyage and no settlers were left behind.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…a set of variations on the song in 1959, on An Evening Wasted with Tom Lehrer, to show his theory that &quot;folk songs are so atrocious because they were written by the people&quot;, singing its verses in the styles of Cole Porter, Mozart, bebop and <b>Gilbert</b> and Sullivan.</blockquote><a href="/songs/oh-my-darling-clementine">Oh My Darling, Clementine</a></li><li><blockquote>Its current form was first published in Carols Ancient and Modern (1823) and in <b>Gilbert</b> and Sandys Carols (1833), collections edited by William Sandys and arranged and edited by Davies Gilbert, who also wrote extra words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-first-nowell">The First Nowell</a></li><li><blockquote>The song is absent from the carol collections of the West-countrymen Davies <b>Gilbert</b> (1822 and 1823) and William Sandys (1833), from the anthologies of Sylvester (1861) and Husk (1864), and from The Oxford Book of Carols (1928).</blockquote><a href="/songs/we-wish-you-a-merry-christmas">We Wish You a Merry Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>At St John's harbour, <b>Humphrey Gilbert</b> claimed Newfoundland for Elizabeth I, an early step in English overseas expansion.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gilbert's</b> son Richard de Clare, Strongbow, led the invasion of Ireland in 1170 from here.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-pembroke">County Palatine of Pembroke</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.7,-3.8&amp;year=1567" aria-label="Devon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Humphrey Gilbert"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4794 5786h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4794 5786h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.7,-3.8&amp;year=1567">What was here in 1567? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are as near to heaven by sea as by land!&#8221;</p><footer>Last words heard from his sinking frigate, 9 September 1583 &#183; original &#183; as Edward Hayes, captain of the Golden Hind, reported them &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3338/pg3338-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
